/*! \page page_json AnCH JSON library documentation
 *
 * \tableofcontents
 *
 * \section json_intro_seq Introduction
 *
 * AnCH JSON library is JSON serialization and deserialization library for JSON format.
 *
 * \section json_prereq_sec Prerequisites
 *
 * AnCH JSON is a standalone library.
 *
 * \section json_install_sec Installation
 *
 * CMake target \c anch::json:
 * \code{.cmake}
find_package(Anch REQUIRED)
target_link_libraries(my-app PRIVATE anch::json)
\endcode
 * See \ref install_sec "installation".
 *
 * \section json_concept_sec Conception
 *
 * Serialized fields are declared by the description of each type shared by formats: an \c anch::Mapping
 * specialization (anch-utils, \c mapping.hpp , see \ref page_utils ) whose \c registerObject function registers the
 * fields with their JSON key and their member pointer (or a getter and / or a setter). \c std::optional , pointers,
 * collections and maps fields are mapped with the mapper of their value type.\n
 * \n
 * \c anch::json::serialize writes JSON in a stream or returns a string ; \c anch::json::deserialize reads a
 * stream and raises \ref anch::json::MappingError on invalid JSON (with its position). Mapping options
 * (\ref anch::json::MappingOptions ) control unknown fields, \c null values and limits (depth, payload size).\n
 * With \c ANCH_UUID and \c ANCH_DATE options, \c anch::UUID , \c anch::date::Date and
 * \c std::chrono::year_month_day are mapped as strings (RFC 3339 for dates).
 *
 * \section json_example_sec Examples
 *
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-json/examples/json.cpp example
 *
 * Output:
 * \code{.txt}
{"id":1,"name":"Rex","tags":["dog","good boy"]}
Médor Vincent
\endcode
 *
 * \section json_required_sec Required fields and error path
 *
 * Constraints can be given when a field is registered. \ref anch::val::required checks on deserialization that
 * the field is present (error code \c MISSING_FIELD , every missing field is listed in the message) and that its
 * value is not \c null , unless the field can hold \c null ( \c std::optional , \c std::shared_ptr ,
 * \c std::unique_ptr or pointer ; error code \c CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION ). Fields which are not required are not
 * checked: a \c null value leaves them unchanged.\n
 * \n
 * \ref anch::json::MappingError::getPath gives the JSON Pointer (RFC 6901) of the value in error, from the
 * deserialized root value ; \c what() starts with this path.
 *
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-json/examples/required.cpp example
 *
 * Output:
 * \code{.txt}
/pets/1 -> /pets/1: Missing required field 'name' near '"Vincent"}]}'
/pets/0/name -> /pets/0/name: Required field can not be null near 'name":null,"owner":n'
\endcode
 *
 * \section json_constraints_sec Value constraints
 *
 * Value constraints of anch-val (see \ref page_val , header \c val/constraints.hpp ) are given at registration too.
 * They are checked after the field value has been read and raise \ref anch::json::MappingError with
 * \c CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION error code. \c null and absent values are not checked ( \ref anch::val::required checks
 * presence). Constraints are checked at compile time against the field type: \c minLength on an integer field does
 * not compile.
 *
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-json/examples/constraints.cpp example
 *
 * Output:
 * \code{.txt}
/tags/1: Length 0 is lower than minLength 1 near '["dog",""]}'
/scores/agility: Value 12 is not a multiple of 5 near 'ility":12}}'
/age: Value -1 is lower than minimum 0 near '","age":-1}'
/color: Value 'brown' is not allowed (allowed: 'black', 'white') near 'r":"brown"}'
\endcode
 *
 * \section json_check_sec Constraints checks on deserialization and serialization
 *
 * Constraints ( \ref anch::val::required and value constraints) are checked according to mapping options:
 * - \c deserialize_check_constraints (default \c true ): after values have been read ;
 * - \c serialize_check_constraints (default \c false ): before values are written. The error has no input
 *   excerpt (message is \c "<path>: <constraint message>" ) and the output contains the JSON written before the
 *   error.
 *
 * Required fields are always written, even when empty: \c null for empty \c std::optional and pointers, \c [] and
 * \c {} for empty containers, whatever \c serialize_null and \c serialize_empty_col options are (the document
 * would be refused by the other side otherwise).\n
 * \n
 * Server side checks what it receives and trusts what it builds (default options). Client side checks what it
 * sends and trusts server responses:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-json/examples/fragments.cpp client options
 *
 * \section json_validate_sec Validation
 *
 * \c anch::val::validate (anch-val, \c val/validation.hpp , see \ref page_val ) checks an object without serializing it:
 * constraints of its fields and of its nested values, every violation is returned with its path. Mapping options are
 * not used.
 *
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-json/examples/validation.cpp example
 *
 * Output:
 * \code{.txt}
/pets/1/name: Length 0 is lower than minLength 1
/pets/1/age: Value -1 is lower than minimum 0
/pets/1/tags/1: Length 0 is lower than minLength 1
\endcode
 */
